[h1:build-gcc [role cyan Get Gcc & Build & Install]]

[h3 Get gcc code:]

```
git clone --depth=1 https://gcc.gnu.org/git/gcc.git SomeLocalDir
```

[h3 SerenityOS]

```
cd SerenityOS-Project-Dir/Ports/gcc
./build.sh
```

[h3 Haiku OS]

Build it.

[h3 Linux]

```
cd SomeLocalDir
./configure --help
./configure \
	--prefix=/gcc \
	--disable-multilib \
	--enable-languages=c++ \
	--enable-stage1-languages=c++ \
	--build=x86_64-linux-gnu \
	--host=x86_64-linux-gnu \
	--target=x86_64-linux-gnu
```
```
make -j8
make install
```

[h3 Windows]

Use mingw64.

[h1:install-from-distribution [role cyan Install From Distribution]]

[h3 SerenityOS]

No gcc distribution.

[h3 Haiku OS]

Gcc is distributed to haiku os image, and it can be installed by pkgman too:

```
pkgman search gcc
pkgman install gcc
```

[h3 Linux]

```
apt install gcc
```

[h3 Windows]

Use mingw64.

[h1:cpp-code-editor [role cyan c++ code Editor]]

Many good editors can be used for writing c++ code.

[h3 Kakoune]

Kakoune can be installed to SerenityOS, Haiku OS, Linux, Windows.

[h3 Featherpad]

Featherpad can be installed to Haiku OS, Linux.

[h3 Native Text Editor]

* SerenityOS: [* Serenity Text Editor]
* Haiku OS: [* Haiku Pe], [* Haiku StyleEdit]
* Linux: [*Kwrite]

[h1:check [role cyan Check compiling]]

Check if the c++ compiler works.

Copy paste the following c++ code, and save as hello.cpp:

[!c++]
```
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
	std::cout << "Hello, c++!" << std::endl;
}
```

Compile and run the c++ code:

```
g++ hello.cpp -std=c++26 -lgcc_s -o hello
./hello
```

It should output:

```
Hello, c++!
```

If the gcc c++ compiler can not compile c++ code, find and repair issues, or ask for help.

